  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Helvellosebacina concrescens is a small, inconspicuous fungus belonging to the Sebacinaceae family, characterized by its gelatinous fruiting bodies that often appear as tiny, translucent structures. This species is primarily known from ecological studies in New England and plays an important role as a mycorrhizal associate with various plant roots.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous Texture",
      "description": "The fruiting bodies are distinctly gelatinous and translucent, typically appearing jelly-like when moist and becoming brittle when dry.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Microscopic Features",
      "description": "Characteristic septa and spore morphology visible under microscopy are essential for definitive identification within the Sebacinaceae family.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies are minute and easily overlooked, typically measuring just a few millimeters in diameter.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Helvellosebacina concrescens is found in moist woodland soils and leaf litter throughout New England, where it forms mycorrhizal associations with the roots of woody plants and understory vegetation.",
  "seasonality": {
